From 67e003dbfa90f13dd1e5bd03fae084908367edb4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sergey Fedorov <barracuda@macos-powerpc.org>
Date: Fri, 23 Oct 2026 03:53:32 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 07/20] fix(cpu): make quant block decoding independent of host
 byte order

Several scalar dequant routines and the ppc64 kernels reinterpret byte
arrays as wider words, or word tables as bytes, assuming a little-endian
host. On a big-endian host they decode wrong values even when the
multi-byte fields of a block are in host order:

- Q5_0/Q5_1: qh is uint8_t[4] holding a little-endian 32-bit mask but was
  memcpy'd into a host-order uint32.
- IQ2_XXS: grid indices were read through a byte view of two host-order
  uint32 words built from the qs uint16 values.
- IQ3_XXS and IQ1_M read byte arrays as host-order uint32/uint16.
- I-quant grids (iq2s/iq2xs/iq2xxs/iq1s as uint64_t, iq3s/iq3xxs as
  uint32_t) hold one grid value per byte, least significant first, but
  were read through byte pointers in memory order (scalar dequant and the
  ppc64 IQ kernels).
- ppc64 Q4_K: the kmask scale unpack memcpy'd the 12 scale bytes into
  host-order words before extracting bytes by value.

Add include/forge/byteorder.h with little-endian load/store helpers and
use them at these sites. On little-endian hosts the helpers are plain
loads/stores and the decoded values are unchanged: a dump of every scalar
dequant routine over random blocks is bit-identical before and after on
x86-64, and identical to that x86-64 dump on 32-bit big-endian PowerPC
(qemu-ppc), where the old code got Q5_0/Q5_1, IQ1_S/IQ1_M, IQ2_*, IQ3_*
wrong. (Q3_K's kmask trick is byte-local, so it needed no change.)

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
